          "question": "I am 68 with COPD and I have been more breathless for three days with green sputum. Is this an emergency, and what does guidance describe about exacerbations?",
          "answer": "**Is this an emergency?** The symptom check found no emergency pattern in its list, and it rated this as routine. That is not a clearance. The check only covers a fixed list of presentations, and it said that if symptoms are severe, getting worse, or new and unexplained, you should seek medical advice. Because you are 68, have COPD, and have had three days of worsening breathlessness, please contact your doctor or a clinician today. If you have severe trouble breathing, struggle to speak in full sentences, feel confused or drowsy, have blue lips or fingers, or your breathing keeps getting worse, call your local emergency number now.\n\n**What the guidance I could retrieve says:**\n- The COPD summary (General clinical reference) describes COPD as a long-term condition with chronic cough and sputum production. It notes that people often first present at an exacerbation, which is a flare-up. It does not describe how exacerbations are recognised, assessed, or treated.\n- The retrieved documents do not describe what counts as a COPD exacerbation, when urgent care is needed, or how it is managed. I could not find that in the sources available to me, so I cannot answer that part of your question from them.\n- The green sputum point came from documents about the common cold and antibiotic use (Upper respiratory infection, and the antibiotic stewardship guidance). They say that green discharge is part of a normal viral cold and is not by itself a sign of bacterial infection. Those documents are about people without COPD, so I cannot say whether that applies to you.\n\nA clinician who knows your history can judge whether this is a COPD flare-up and what to do next.\n\n*Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.*",
